WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.689 --> 00:00:04.100
Ever since he was first introduced in the
mid-credits scene at the end of the first

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:08.029
Avengers film, Thanos has loomed large in
the imaginations of Marvel fans.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:12.260
Now that both Avengers: Infinity War and Avengers:
Endgame have come and gone, it's clear that

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:16.460
the masterminds behind the Marvel Cinematic
Universe had big plans for Thanos going back

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:17.460
years.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:20.850
But just what those plans were definitely
changed from that first appearance and in

00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:24.230
the case of Guardians of the Galaxy, they
apparently changed quite a bit.

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:28.500
According to Charlie Wen, former head of visual
development for Marvel Studios, there were

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:32.529
plans to make Thanos a much bigger part of
the first cosmic movie in the MCU.

00:00:32.529 --> 00:00:34.649
He explained to us via email:

00:00:34.649 --> 00:00:36.780
"Thanos' role would have been significantly
larger.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:40.329
We would have seen some of the relationship
[and] tensions play out between him and his

00:00:40.329 --> 00:00:44.359
kids [...] Ronan's role would have decreased,
with Thanos taking on more."

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:48.179
Wen was responsible for bringing the earliest
versions of Thanos from the comic book page

00:00:48.179 --> 00:00:49.379
to the big screen.

00:00:49.379 --> 00:00:53.670
And since the Mad Titan was going to be a
major player for the growing MCU early on,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:57.410
Wen knew he had to get the details right to
communicate that threat at a glance.

00:00:57.410 --> 00:01:02.820
"Early on, Thanos was setting up to be a major
climax for the MCU, but the first time I designed

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:04.890
Thanos was for his first appearance in Avengers.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:09.500
His armor had to reflect the look of his iconic
comic book origins (while being more practical)

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:12.140
as well as a nod to his Chitauri soldiers
in the film.

00:01:12.140 --> 00:01:16.380
"Because he was going to play such a defining
role in the MCU later, it was important to

00:01:16.380 --> 00:01:21.290
capture the look that fans know of him: an
enormous bulky purple frame, bluish fabric,

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:25.360
complemented by a golden armor in the shape
of a slight crescent moon accentuating his

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:29.890
shoulders, leading up to large slits along
his chin, and finally capped by a helmet that

00:01:29.890 --> 00:01:32.530
reflects the shape language of his body armor."

00:01:32.530 --> 00:01:37.180
But as time went on, Avengers writer-director
Joss Whedon reportedly made the call to diminish

00:01:37.180 --> 00:01:42.180
Thanos's role in the early stages of the MCU
to make for a more satisfying build-up over

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:43.180
time.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:47.010
That meant changes to work being done on Guardians
of the Galaxy during pre-production.

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:50.480
And then, after making those changes, making
even more changes.

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:51.480
Wen explained:

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.520
"It was decided to cut Thanos out of Guardians
completely in order to have a slower reveal

00:01:55.520 --> 00:01:56.520
of him.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.860
It wasn't until right before the shoot that
I was told that Thanos would have a role in

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:02.920
the film, so designing him and his throne
was pretty rushed.

00:02:02.920 --> 00:02:05.490
[...] I only had a couple days left to design
him on his throne.

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:09.830
I made some improvements from the first design,
in which we saw the side of his face."

00:02:09.830 --> 00:02:13.770
As for the throne itself, it's fascinating
to look at some of Wen's concept art from

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:18.200
throughout his tenure at Marvel Studios to
see how the MCU's plans for Thanos and his

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.200
throne evolved.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:23.910
For instance, in two concept images that Wen
labeled "Ronan Ceremony," we see the Mad Titan

00:02:23.910 --> 00:02:28.450
holding court on a rocky throne, with Ronan
and Nebula standing before him as an audience

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:29.700
of aliens watch.

00:02:29.700 --> 00:02:31.890
But that's not quite the scene we get in the
film.

00:02:31.890 --> 00:02:36.010
When Nebula and Ronan appear before Thanos,
there's no audience, and Thanos's throne is

00:02:36.010 --> 00:02:37.450
markedly different.

00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:41.590
As to where Wen got the idea to create the
floating platforms that made up Thanos's throne,

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:45.670
the answer lies in concept art he did for
a different Marvel Studios film just a few

00:02:45.670 --> 00:02:48.350
years earlier: the very first Thor feature.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:52.460
Since that movie was so early in the MCU's
evolution, Wen worked hard to find as many

00:02:52.460 --> 00:02:57.330
different takes on the movie's important characters
as he could and that included this very sci-fi

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.500
influenced version of Thor's dad, Odin.

00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:03.680
That throne design didn't make it into Thor,
but it seems to have found a second life under

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:05.820
Thanos for Guardians of the Galaxy.

00:03:05.820 --> 00:03:08.569
But enough about Thanos's butt what about
his face?

00:03:08.569 --> 00:03:12.230
Even though Josh Brolin played the character
in the first Guardians of the Galaxy film,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:16.209
it's easy to see that the character didn't
really resemble the actor very much at least,

00:03:16.209 --> 00:03:18.980
not to the extent that the Thanos of Infinity
War did.

00:03:18.980 --> 00:03:22.450
There's a reason for that, explained Wen:
during the pre-production phase on Guardians

00:03:22.450 --> 00:03:25.459
of the Galaxy, Brolin hadn't yet been cast
in the part.

00:03:25.459 --> 00:03:28.930
"It was on Infinity War that Josh Brolin's
face needed to be incorporated.

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:34.200
By then, I'd left Marvel, and my partner in
crime, and co-head of Marvel's Visual Development

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.840
Ryan Meinerding, did the honors and designed
the version in that film."

00:03:37.840 --> 00:03:42.099
With Avengers: Endgame setting records and
revealing Thanos's fate to fans all over the

00:03:42.099 --> 00:03:46.160
world, it's exciting to wonder how different
things could've been if Marvel Studios had

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:50.420
stuck with their initial plans to give Thanos
a bigger presence in the original Guardians

00:03:50.420 --> 00:03:51.480
of the Galaxy.

00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:53.580
Would he have ended up making as big of an
impact?

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:56.569
To that, Marvel fans can only say "What if…"

